# pots v1.4 — a standing heart rate observation, on its own.
#
# WHY STANDALONE. Cascade validates a pod file by file, and
# pots:HeartRateMeasurementShape targets the class rather than a path from a
# check result, so a measurement written to a different file from the check it
# belongs to arrives exactly like this. The same argument
# clinical/encounter-participant-standalone.VALID.ttl makes for
# clinical:EncounterParticipant.
#
# It also isolates the shape. potscheck-nasalean-full.VALID.ttl contains four of
# these, so a failure there could belong to any of five shapes; a failure here
# belongs to this one.
#
# THE VALUE IS REACHED BY A SEQUENCE PATH, which is the part worth knowing.
# pots:HeartRateMeasurementShape's second Violation constraint has
# sh:path ( fhir:valueQuantity fhir:value ) -- a two-step path into the nested
# quantity node, not a property on the measurement itself. A fixture that put the
# number directly on the measurement would satisfy neither that constraint nor
# the sh:minCount 1 on fhir:valueQuantity above it, and would fail with two
# violations rather than zero.
#
# 104 bpm at one minute upright: a real standing value for someone whose supine
# baseline is in the seventies, and comfortably inside the shape's 20-300 range
# without sitting at a boundary. The out-of-range case is
# heartrate-above-range.INVALID.ttl.
#
# Fully synthetic.

@prefix pots: <https://ns.cascadeprotocol.org/pots/v1#> .
@prefix fhir: <http://hl7.org/fhir/> .
@prefix loinc: <http://loinc.org/rdf#> .
@prefix ucum: <http://unitsofmeasure.org/> .
@prefix xsd: <http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#> .

<urn:uuid:b0750002-0000-4a00-8000-000000001010> a pots:HeartRateMeasurement ;
    fhir:code loinc:8867-4 ;
    fhir:valueQuantity [
        fhir:value "104.0"^^xsd:double ;
        fhir:unit "beats/minute" ;
        fhir:code ucum:min-1
    ] ;
    pots:secondsSinceStand "60.0"^^xsd:double ;
    pots:atSeconds "60"^^xsd:integer .
